2186229133 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229133 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186229982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1050 1440.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229257 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ecc6f7d5-a6a0-5418-9648-573635651982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby substituting \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de398203-cf28-58bc-baf8-d4c0b37651f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229683 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nin $\\text{L2}$ and substituting ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t)$ from the original system gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$, $\\text{L5}$, and $\\text{L6}.$ From $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\ny(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nUsing $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ from $\\text{L5}$, this becomes\n\n$$\ny(t)-x({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substituting $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0}).$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L1}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t) \\\\  & =x(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229982 #edb/ref 1061 589676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e2fe55d-42e2-5c03-89a2-e19a648504d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229983 #edb/ref 1061 589677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77745a11-4271-51f5-b398-2f10162b46cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229984 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229984 #edb/ref 1061 589680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229984 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6314ffb3-c936-5a5a-b8eb-f9a041a7e1a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229984 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229985 #edb/ref 1061 589683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229985 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96c720fc-5ade-5920-b439-4021bbbc0586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229985 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230191 #edb/ref 1064 "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving *inhomogeneous systems* of ODEs as well. Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, and suppose we are given an initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\n\nOur goal is to derive the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the *inhomogeneous term* by a change of variables. We define\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand differentiate it using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =\\cancel{-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+\\cancel{A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate both sides from $t_0$ to $t$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to the initial variable $\\mathbf{x}(t).$ To do that we multiply both sides by $e^{A(t-t_0)}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our IVP is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere the first term represents the solution of the corresponding *homogeneous system*, and the second term gives a particular solution of our *inhomogeneous system*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230191 #edb/ref 1065 14524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230191 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff1bfa7f-286f-5481-8170-effabae1aa53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230191 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306955 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\operatorname{sin}(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}(2t)+3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306955 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306955 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c139fc5d-402e-5c97-b048-caf60e14b36c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307958 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b933995-aeb2-5960-92c8-2edb1a0843a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308813 #edb/ref 1080 "applying a change of variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308813 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308813 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08a0e04b-fdf3-54cb-b350-9898e7a963ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309170 #edb/ref 1080 "applying a change of variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "373d3c75-9124-5814-aa1b-1a43605c0101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309173 #edb/ref 1080 "evaluating at $t=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309173 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #e